Under Assumption <a href="#intro:characteristic" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="intro:characteristic">1</a>, for every prime $\ell\ne\operatorname{char}(\mathbb F_q)$ and every closed invariant $Y\subset\widehat{\mathcal N}$, the natural map $$\label{intro:main-map}
E\otimes_\mathbb Q\bigl(\mathcal A_\mathbb Q\cap\mathcal F_Y\mathcal A_E\bigr)
\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\mathcal F_Y\mathcal A_E$$ is an isomorphism. The intersection is taken inside $\mathcal A_E$.

Every connected component $Z_\sigma$ of the restricted local-system stack has a unique semisimple isomorphism class $\sigma$. Its automorphism group $J=\mathop{\mathrm{Aut}}(\sigma)$ is reductive, possibly disconnected. There is a formal affine coarse space $S_\sigma$ with reduced space a point, and $Z_\sigma\to S_\sigma$ is relatively algebraic. After choosing a frame at a geometric point, the component is formal affine. For an affine infinitesimal stage $S_n\to S_\sigma$, its base change is affine; almost finite type stages and bounded Postnikov truncations may be used. These affine stages have coherent cohomology in each bounded range. The tangent complex at a local system $\tau$ is $$\label{inp:tangent}
 T_\tau\mathop{\mathrm{LocSys}}^{\mathrm{restr}}_{\widehat G}(\overline X)
       \simeq R\Gamma(\overline X,\widehat{\mathfrak g}_\tau)[1].$$ In particular the stack is quasi-smooth.

For $P,P'\in\mathsf E_e$, the Hom complex between the orbit generators has a finite equivariant calculation with Euler terms $$\label{orb:hom-euler}
 \left[
 \mathbb C[\ker(e:V\to V)]\otimes
 \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}_{D_e}(P,P')\otimes
 \Lambda^\bullet(N_e[-1])\otimes
 \Lambda^\bullet(\mathop{\mathrm{Lie}}(U_e)^*[-1])
 \right]^{L_e}.$$ Here the coefficient factor has lifted degree zero; the shifts in the normal and Lie factors add one to the degrees in <a href="#orb:weight-table" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="orb:weight-table">[orb:weight-table]</a>. The formula is an equality of alternating equivariant characters, and a finite-filtration calculation bounding weights, rather than an assertion that the Hom complex splits as the displayed tensor product.*


*For pure coefficient normalizations, all nonconstant terms have strictly negative absolute $S_e$-weight. The weight-zero cohomology is $$\label{orb:weight-zero-hom}
 H^\bullet\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}(M_P,M_{P'})_{\mathrm{wt}=0}
       =\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}_{D_e,L_e}(P,P')[0],$$ with ordinary composition. Before the finite invariant calculation, weight pieces are finite-dimensional and their dimensions grow at most polynomially over bounded-length weight intervals. In particular, the character sums in <a href="#orb:hom-euler" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="orb:hom-euler">[orb:hom-euler]</a> are absolutely convergent on every normalized compact $L_e$-coset, uniformly on that coset.

For every Frobenius-stable conical $z$-support bound $\Omega$, inclusion induces an isomorphism $$\label{tr:remove-completion}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}(\Phi,\operatorname{Ind}\mathscr B_{\Omega,0})
       \xrightarrow{\;\sim\;}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}(\Phi,\operatorname{Ind}\mathscr B_\Omega).$$ A compact Weil object on the right has a compact Weil lift on the left whose trace class maps to a nonzero scalar multiple of its class. Both statements hold for the compact opposite categories. Moreover, for Hom pairings of two such lifts, the finite Koszul construction multiplies each orbit contribution by a constant nonzero factor, independent of the compact stabilizer variable.

For every $f,g\in\mathcal A_{\mathbb C}$ there is a unique finite complex Borel measure $\nu_{f,g}$ on $B$ such that, for every finite-dimensional representation $D$ of $\widehat G$, $$\label{meas:moments}
 [T_Df,g]=\int_B\chi_D\,d\nu_{f,g}.$$ For every closed invariant subset $Y\subset\widehat{\mathcal N}$, $$\label{meas:criterion}
 f\in\mathcal F_Y\mathcal A_{\mathbb C}
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 \operatorname{supp}(\nu_{f,g})\subset B_Y
 \quad\text{for every }g\in\mathcal A_{\mathbb C}.$$

Keep the curve $X/\mathbb F_q$, the split connected semisimple group $G/\mathbb F_q$, and all four conditions of Assumption <a href="#intro:characteristic" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="intro:characteristic">1</a>. Put $p=\operatorname{char}(\mathbb F_q)$ and $E_\ell=\overline{\mathbb Q}_\ell$ for $\ell\ne p$. At unramified level $D=0$, let $C_{0,\mathbb Q}\subset\mathcal A_\mathbb Q$ be the cuspidal function space and $C_{0,\mathcal O,\mathbb Q}$ its rational orbit summands from [[CuspidalArthur](#ref-CuspidalArthur), Theorem 1.1], and set $C_{0,\ell}=E_\ell\otimes_\mathbb QC_{0,\mathbb Q}$. For each $\ell\ne p$, let $Y_\ell\subset\widehat{\mathcal N}_{E_\ell}$ be closed and invariant, with the same nilpotent-orbit subset $\mathscr Y$ under the fixed pinned split rational dual group. Write $Y=(Y_\ell)_{\ell\ne p}$ and define $$F^{\mathbb Q}_{Y,\ell}:=\mathcal A_\mathbb Q\cap\mathcal F_{Y_\ell}\mathcal A_{E_\ell},
 \qquad
 V_{Y,\mathbb Q}:=\bigoplus_{\mathcal O\in\mathscr Y}C_{0,\mathcal O,\mathbb Q}.$$ Then $$\label{desc:cuspidal-equalities}
 \begin{split}
 C_{0,\mathbb Q}\cap F^{\mathbb Q}_{Y,\ell}&=V_{Y,\mathbb Q},\\
 C_{0,\ell}\cap\mathcal F_{Y_\ell}\mathcal A_{E_\ell}
   &=E_\ell\otimes_\mathbb QV_{Y,\mathbb Q}.
 \end{split}$$ The intersections are taken in the corresponding finitely supported function spaces. The equalities include the zero cusp space and empty or full $Y_\ell$.
